{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"body":"Plan-review bake-off session, 2026-09-15. Wave 3 completed and Wave 4 executed end to end. No financial figures below, per the local-only-report convention; token counts and wall-clock only.\n\n## Wave 3 — completed\nAll 20 runs judged and adjudicated (three independent judges each, majority aggregation). Zero contested items in any run. The four-arm set finished at 11 runs (2 original + 9 continuation); the other 9 are three-arm with rebar judged separately via supplement panels.\n\nFinal four-arm figures, 11 runs, 343 confirmed issues in the union: CE 271 distinct issues / 99.5% precision / 63.0% recall / 128 sole finds; superpowers 175 / 99.3% / 40.8% / 49; mini 87 / 94.9% / 21.6% / 26; rebar pooled 137 / 86.7%, rebar on-plan 127 / 95.1% / 22.7% / 27. All figures are ceilings per METHODOLOGY.md.\n\n## Scaffolding partition — resolved on judge evidence\nThe unit that matters is not \"mentions scaffolding\" but whether a defect in the pinned plan survives deleting the scaffolding-derived ground. Four-arm set: 10 issues excluded, precision 86.7% -> 95.1%. Supplement set: 1 item excluded, 93.2% -> 94.5%. Two independent panels converging on ~94-95% is a genuine consistency check. Determinations recorded machine-readably in harness/scaffold_only.json; from rb-spree onward judges tag rows with an explicit SCAFFOLD-ANCHORED ONLY|SURVIVES token, and all three judges agreed unanimously on that run.\n\n## Corrections to my own earlier claims\n- The \"7/7 ac_source predictor\" does not hold in strong form. ac_source predicts exposure, not incidence; java-iceberg is copied:recorded-selection yet has zero scaffolding-anchored rows.\n- The supplement contamination alarm was overstated and is RETRACTED. 14 of 15 flagged rows survive the deletion test, including all five gutenberg rows. Published 93.2% moves to 94.5%, not materially.\n- java-gradle's \"0% precision\" was our harness, not rebar. All five of its false positives are scaffolding-anchored.\n- FOURARM_RESULTS.md's \"defensible vs competitive\" explanation for the 93.2%-vs-86% gap is SUPERSEDED. On the on-plan basis the two sets are 94.5% and 95.1% — the gap was our own appended text, not a design difference.\n- I wrote a false claim into FOURARM_RESULTS.md that per-category kappa on fp is \"markedly higher than nit throughout\". It is 5 of 10 runs. Corrected in place.\n\n## Instrument defects found\n- JUDGE_INSTRUCTIONS_W4.md states NO rule for classifying a scaffolding-anchored finding. Judges invented two incompatible ones (opensearch -> fp, mn-deed/signal-coverage -> nit), which moves rebar's headline precision. Resolved by partitioning, not by forcing a class.\n- A per-run framing direction (the opensearch RFC exemption) existed only in an unrecorded dispatch prompt. Recorded retroactively; it was document-grounded and correct.\n- I introduced a bias into the rb-spree dispatch prompt and withdrew it ~3 minutes in, replacing a class directive with a neutral labelling request.\n\n## Corpus and environment defects\n- Wave 1 checkouts have had NO working git for the entire study. They are worktrees whose metadata was pruned; `git log` fails outright. Every arm instruction file permits git log/show.\n- Wave 3 checkouts are SHALLOW single-commit clones. Commands run but there is no history. So NO run in either wave could ever ground a claim in history. Symmetric across arms, so no comparison is distorted, but it bounds what \"code-grounded\" means here.\n- 36ab-b649-BLOCK's plan is genuinely corrupt IN THE PINNED SOURCE TICKET (108 BLE001 lines, 26 literal \\n escapes, ~74% unrelated transcript), matching the recorded desc_chars exactly. The live ticket has since been cleaned upstream. Nothing needs re-running; every arm saw the same document. rebar's precheck refusal was CORRECT and the study describes it as \"produced nothing\".\n\n## Wave 4 — superpowers on Sonnet, all 40 plans, COMPLETE\nPurpose: separate the skill from the model it ran on. superpowers 6.3.0, identical to the Opus-5 arm in every respect except model. 40/40 runs, all status ok, all checkouts clean, 40/40 token and timing records. 11,963,309 tokens; 4.82h review wall-clock (self-clock) / 5.57h agent wall-clock. Wave 1 median 487s vs Wave 3 median 416s.\n\nInstrumented before kickoff at the operator's instruction: two independent clocks (agent self-stamp measuring the review; harness duration_ms measuring the agent lifetime) plus per-run tokens. The two clocks diverge by a VARIABLE 50-108s and are not interconvertible.\n\nFirst Wave 4 result, and it arrived before any finding count: the same skill with IDENTICAL instructions shows 7.5% vs 40% compliance with the reviewer prompt's own dispatch-a-subagent directive (Opus 3/40, Sonnet 16/40). Attributable to the model — the instruction sentence is byte-identical in both instruction files. It is also a CONFOUND: dispatched runs get two passes of attention, so any recall gain is partly the extra pass. Ledger records which path each run took.\n\n## Model provenance — established, and a trap corrected\nverdict[\"model\"] is NOT the review model. It reports the LAST model used (the Sonnet coach). rebar's reviewer actually runs on Opus-4-8 at the frontier class. I walked into this trap and corrected it. Full picture: rebar reviewer Opus-4-8; CE and superpowers Opus 5 (session default, CE tiering some personas down to Haiku/Sonnet); mini Sonnet-4-6; Wave 4 superpowers Sonnet. mini's recall is therefore measured on a materially weaker model — a confound that should travel with its numbers.\n\n## Artifacts written\nARMS_PROVENANCE.md (model provenance + popularity/health for the three third-party arms, and the candidate-rejection table); harness/fourarm_report.py (regenerates all four-arm tables from adjudications; verified idempotent); harness/scaffold_only.json; harness/runs_wave4.json; harness/SP_INSTRUCTIONS_W4.md; harness/wave4_ledger.py + wave4_ledger.json; wave3/METRIC4.md; reports/aggregate-findings.html (published artifact); several FIDELITY_LOG.md entries.\n\n## Fifth/sixth arm research\nThree candidates evaluated, all unsuitable: addyosmani/agent-skills (0 of 25 skills review a plan), Agensi Change Plan Reviewer (no codebase input, unpinnable, paywalled, 0 installs), AgentSkills.me (no candidate; its closest hit IS our superpowers arm). gstack plan-ceo-review passes the codebase bar but fails on interactivity (AskUserQuestion is its control flow) and counts approvals rather than findings. Its sibling plan-eng-review is unevaluated and is the remaining lead. Recurring finding: plan-review-against-a-codebase barely exists as a category.\n\n## Bugs filed\nDuplication bug consolidated onto the PRE-EXISTING longish-ripe-snowyowl (2331-3e0e-f880-4837), extended from 2 runs to 11: 164 emitted -> 137 distinct (+19.7%), worst case rb-spree +67%, 24 duplicate groups, distinct finding ids on every genuine pair, three drop reasons none of which is deduplication, 28 blocking member findings. I filed a DUPLICATE ticket by mistake (arbored-demurrable-whippet, now closed with class=duplicate and linked) because I filtered a capped rebar search result set and treated it as the corpus. A negative rebar search result is not proof of absence.\n\n## Recurring defect class, now ~12 instances\nA filter that was correct for the data in hand and silently wrong because the data was not all the data: an aggregator glob, a .rebar contamination scan, a wave-scoped inventory, a && chain, a len() on a dict, a three-check preflight grep, ballot.py's wave-3 rebar drop, the scaffolding keyword detector (twice, opposite directions), a capped rebar search, and a hand-typed language label in a dispatch prompt.\n\n## Open decisions at termination\n1. How to judge Wave 4 (full 5-arm re-judge ~50M tokens and invalidates 40 adjudications, vs judging against the existing reference set). Recommended the latter.\n2. Evaluate gstack's plan-eng-review sibling, or stop at five arms. Recommended evaluating.\n3. 36ab corpus: leave frozen (recommended) vs edit; either way the study's description of that run should change.\n4. Sonnet[1M] could not be pinned via the dispatch tool; runs used plain Sonnet. Recommended accepting.\n5. Wave 2 leftovers: recommended shelving Q3, resuming Q1.\n6. Coverage panel scope: four-arm 265 issues (recommended) vs all 453.\n\n## Not yet done\nWave 4 is UNJUDGED. Wave 3 coverage panel is approved but blocked on adapting coverage_pack.py (reads runs.json and rebar_findings.json, which cover 0 of 11 Wave 3 runs; Wave 3 adjudication headers are null). Bedrock dollar conversion requested but not performed. Metric 4 computed for Wave 3 but not folded into the published artifact. Wave 1 vs Wave 3 side-by-side comparison not built."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789514219844883001,"uuid":"c9b5c7d5-7bd1-45e0-a65f-b10477954743"}